BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Prompt Sounds and Effects with AI Tools
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T200000
UID:69a202eb8089f23c675eb01c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Musicians and producers spend hours tweaking knobs and sliders
  to get the perfect sound. But what if you could simply describe the sound
  you're looking for - "warm and fuzzy\," "sharp and metallic\," "underwate
 r echo" - and have AI create it for you?\nA new generation of AI-powered t
 ools is making this possible\, letting you build custom sounds and effects
  using plain language descriptions right inside your music software. In th
 is session\, you'll learn the vocabulary that helps AI understand what you
 're after\, then put these tools to the test to see how close they can get
  to the sound in your head. No prior music or programming experience is re
 quired.\nRegistration is required.\nThis program is part of the Innovator 
 in Residence (AI and Music Production) program\, which is designed to help
  beginner and intermediate learners build knowledge and creative skills an
 d get hands-on with current AI tools in music production and learn about t
 he history\, copyright and ethics of generative music. For more informatio
 n and a full list of programs\, visit\ntpl.ca/airesident\n.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Digital Innovation Hub\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a202eb8089f23c675eb01c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Classic Twists: The Turn of the Screw
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T130000
UID:69dd16264dacf581ff887b85@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:"The story had held us\, round the fire\, sufficiently breathl
 ess . . ."\nSince its publication in 1898\,\nThe Turn of the Screw\nhas be
 guiled generations of readers\, and inspired countless adaptations. A dece
 ptively simple story\, in which the governess of two boys at an English co
 untry house becomes convinced that her wards are haunted\, this taut gothi
 c novella challenges readers to determine the borderline of the paranormal
  and the imagination. Wrapped up in its gripping\, page-turning narrative 
 are fundamental questions about the nature of reality\, and whether the tr
 ue haunting comes from restless ghosts or from the unresolved energies of 
 memory and history.\nIn this next instalment of Toronto Public Library's s
 eries on classic literature\, host Rebecca Panovka is joined by acclaimed 
 novelist Colm Tóibín and editor Kiara Barrow to tease out the ambiguous\
 , endlessly generative threads of this supreme masterpiece.\nABOUT THIS EV
 ENT'S GUESTS:\nColm Tóibín\nwas born in Enniscorthy in 1955. He is the a
 uthor of 11 novels including\nThe Master\, Brooklyn\, The Testament of Mar
 y\, Nora Webster\, House of Names\nand\nThe Magician\n. His work has been 
 shortlisted for The Booker Prize three times\, has won the Costa Novel Awa
 rd and the IMPAC Award. He has also published two collections of stories a
 nd many works of non-fiction.\nKiara Barrow\nis cofounder and coeditor of\
 nThe Drift\nand a senior editor at Penguin Press.\nRebecca Panovka\nis a f
 ounding editor of\nThe Drift\n.\nHer essays and criticism have appeared in
 \nHarper's\, The New Yorker\, The New York Times\, Bookforum\, Dissent\, T
 he Paris Review\, The Drift\n\, and elsewhere.\n***\nImportant information
  for accessing this live virtual event:\nYou must register for a free tick
 et via Eventbrite to gain access to this event.\nThis event will be presen
 ted in a Zoom Webinar. Once registered\, you will receive reminder emails 
 from both Zoom and Eventbrite with links and instructions to access your u
 nique Zoom Webinar link to watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, y
 ou must be signed in to Zoom or Eventbrite with the same email address tha
 t you used to book your ticket in order to access your unique event link. 
 Unique event links can only be open on one device at a time.\n***\nThis ev
 ent is made possible in part from a gift from the Ann Thoburn Fund.\n***\n
 Accessibility at Toronto Public Library\nWe are committed to making our co
 ntent accessible to as many Torontonians as possible. Please call or email
  us if you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request accommo
 dation to participate in this program. Please let us know as far in advanc
 e as possible and we will do our best to meet your request. At least three
  weeks' notice is preferred. Phone 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservice
 s@tpl.ca
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69dd16264dacf581ff887b85
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
