BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Julia Hotz: The Connection Cure
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260622T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260622T200000
UID:69cd822b8089f23c67660d6d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:How do we build communities that prescribe connection rather t
 han isolation?\nIn her groundbreaking book\nThe Connection Cure: The Presc
 riptive Power of Movement\, Nature\, Art\, Service\, and Belonging\,\njour
 nalist Julia Hotz reveals a revolutionary approach to health that's spread
 ing across over thirty countries. Instead of defaulting to pharmaceuticals
 \, doctors and healthcare workers are writing prescriptions for community 
 activities. The results are remarkable\, patients reporting dramatic impro
 vements not just in symptoms\, but in their sense of purpose\, connection\
 , and joy.\nWhat role can public institutions like libraries play in becom
 ing hubs of social health? How do we shift from treating symptoms of sickn
 ess to reconnecting people with sources of wellness? And what would it mea
 n if we redesigned our cities\, our healthcare systems\, and our daily rou
 tines around the radical idea that human connection is medicine?\nJoin one
  of the leading voices on social prescribing for a conversation about tran
 sforming how we think about health\, community\, and what it means to trul
 y care for one another.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available f
 or purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nJulia Hotz\nTara Henley\nRead 
 more:\nThe Connection Cure\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Blu
 ma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\
 nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registrat
 ion required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the
  greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guarante
 ed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first s
 erved\nBar:\nBeer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\nAccess
 ibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a dis
 ability and would like to request accommodation for this event\, please co
 ntact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior
  to the event.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\,
  where we host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in c
 onversation about their new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collect
 ion Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Tor
 onto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c
 )\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program reg
 istration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill 
 out a brief survey following the program. Questions about how the library 
 handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline
 @tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69cd822b8089f23c67660d6d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dystopian Fiction In a Dystopian Present
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260603T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260603T200000
UID:69d7adc78ddc1caf5f1bc962@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Author\nRachel A. Rosen\n("Cascade"\; "So Human As I Am") will
  explore the intersection of politics with sci-fi and fantasy\, and how au
 thors of speculative fiction navigate writing dystopian and cautionary tal
 es when the present threatens to eclipse fiction.\nnformation Collection N
 otice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto 
 Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, a
 nd 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registra
 tion\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles yo
 ur information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n
 \, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69d7adc78ddc1caf5f1bc962
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Write AI Prompts to Get the Music You Want
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260622T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260622T180000
UID:69a2043c491b809c6f1cfe03@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Describing music in words has always been a challenge - what e
 xactly does "warm" or "dreamy" or "punchy" sound like? When you're using A
 I to create music\, the words you choose make all the difference between g
 etting something magical and getting something that misses the mark entire
 ly.\nIn this session\, you'll discover why music is so hard to describe\, 
 then put that knowledge to work by experimenting with different prompting 
 techniques to see how accurately you can guide AI toward the sound in your
  head. You'll also learn advanced strategies\, including how to use one AI
  to help you write better instructions for another. No prior music or prog
 ramming experience is required.\nRegistration is required.\nThis program i
 s part of the Innovator in Residence (AI and Music Production) program\, w
 hich is designed to help beginner and intermediate learners build knowledg
 e and creative skills and get hands-on with current AI tools in music prod
 uction and learn about the history\, copyright and ethics of generative mu
 sic. For more information and a full list of programs\, visit tpl.ca/aires
 ident.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Digital Innovation Hub\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a2043c491b809c6f1cfe03
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Obstacle is the Phone: Stoic Focus in a Distracted Digital Wor
 ld
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260617T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260617T200000
UID:69dfc8391e64afd01e6962be@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Navigate today’s fast‑paced digital world with clarity and
  confidence. In an era of constant pings\, pop‑ups\, and pressure to sta
 y connected\, our attention is pulled in every direction—yet most advice
  focuses on screen‑time guilt rather than building real inner skills.\nT
 his interactive session offers a fresh approach grounded in the Stoic disc
 iplines of Perception\, Action\, and Will. Through practical strategies\, 
 you’ll explore how to notice digital pulls clearly\, respond with intent
 ion\, and stay aligned with your values amid everyday digital noise.\nInfo
 rmation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is colle
 cted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sectio
 ns 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event 
 and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how th
 e library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\
 nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G
 8.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69dfc8391e64afd01e6962be
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
