BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Searching Colours for the Stories by Marina Bu
 rke
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260401T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260429T235959
UID:69b02ade4a9fd98b7f8349ac@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of April\, the works of artist Marina Bur
 ke are on display at Leaside branch in an exhibit titled Searching Colours
  for the Stories.\nMarina is a visual artist who lives and creates in Toro
 nto. She has been an active member of The Artists' Network in the East end
  of the city. She likes to experiment with art thus living in a constant 
 search for the right medium to depict the world inside and out.\n"I am con
 stantly evolving as an artist and as a person. The river of life brings th
 e feeling of flow and it is a fascinating ability to experience it. I like
  depicting people and their faces\, trying to reach out to their inner sel
 f\, to capture their spirits\, and\, in the end\, expressing my own feelin
 gs and visions of each and every model that I have."\nThis exhibit is part
  of\nTPL's Art Exhibit\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for
  local artists to exhibit their work at locations across the city. These e
 xhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its 
 neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during\nLeaside Branch\nopen ho
 urs. Please check the Leaside Branch page before your visit.\nFree. All ar
 e welcome.
LOCATION:Leaside\, Community Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69b02ade4a9fd98b7f8349ac
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:La Rosa Enflorece: Traditional Ladino (Judeo-Spanish) Folktales
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260604T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260604T193000
UID:69ecf144129f1274383ac6c8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:After their 1492 expulsion from Spain\, Sephardic Jews settled
  across the Ottoman Empire\, carrying their medieval Spanish language into
  a melting pot of Turkish\, Greek\, Arabic\, Persian\, Bulgarian\, and Fr
 ench influences. This hybrid language\, Ladino\, formed the basis of their
  business\, stories\, and songs for over five centuries. Though Ladino is 
 disappearing from daily use\, the spirit of its rich\, oral tradition rema
 ins.\nJoin oral storyteller\, Ariel Balevi\, for a celebration of Sephardi
 c folktales\, evoking a world where the mystical and the earthly\, and the
  elegiac and the humorous intertwine.\nSpace is limited\; online registrat
 ion is encouraged.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal informati
 on on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Publi
 c Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will
  only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation
 . Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed
  to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Stree
 t\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ecf144129f1274383ac6c8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Maya LeMaitre
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260502T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260530T235959
UID:69ea468ab6c4ac1fedce40df@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout May\, works by artist Maya LeMaitre are on display
  at Mimico Centennial branch.\nMaya is an illustrator\, cartoonist and art
  instructor living in Toronto. She has created several comics and zines\, 
 including a graphic novel which was published in 2022. Maya was born in Mo
 ntreal and grew up in Cape town\, South Africa.\nMaya works mainly in draw
 ing and painting. She is interested in the experience of living in a parti
 cular place--with its intersecting layers of history\, nature\, and person
 al experience.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibits Space\nprogram
 . Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to exhibit their
  work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the dive
 rse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit ca
 n be viewed during\nMimico Centennial\nbranch open hours.\nFree. All are w
 elcome.
LOCATION:Mimico Centennial\, Mezzanine
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ea468ab6c4ac1fedce40df
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Starting and Sustaining a Business (Business Law)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260721T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260721T190000
UID:69d542c7f29b90618efda044@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Calling all business owners and entrepreneurs! Growing your bu
 siness takes hard work and perseverance. Don't let legalities hold you bac
 k. In this presentation\, we will discuss the proper approach under Ontari
 o business law. From incorporation\, to contracts\, to business structurin
 g\, we will go over everything you need to know so you can stay focused on
  what your business does best.\nThis presentation will be led by Samuel Mi
 chaels\, a Toronto-based corporate lawyer who has been running his own law
  firm\, SM Legal\, since 2017.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69d542c7f29b90618efda044
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Between the Lines Bookclub
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T200000
UID:6962837f91e4576f847bd0ff@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Become a page-turner by joining the Between the Lines Book Clu
 b! Here\, we embark on a new chapter with every read\, diving into a world
  of endless possibilities. From gripping thrillers to heartwarming coming-
 of-age tales\, or a sprinkle of fantasy adventures\, there's something for
  everyone. Whether you're a die-hard fan of one genre or love mixing thing
 s up\, you'll find a place here to discuss\, share\, and connect with fell
 ow readers. Ready to explore different worlds and discover your next favor
 ite book? Join us and let?s turn the page together!\nFriday May 15:\nA Man
  Called Ove by Fredrik Backman\nA Man Called Ove by Fredrik Backman is a 
 heartwarming tale about a curmudgeonly\, solitary man named Ove\, whose ri
 gid routines and no-nonsense attitude mask a deep sense of loss and loneli
 ness. When a young family moves in next door\, their unintentional involve
 ment in his life slowly begins to unravel his hardened exterior. Through a
  series of touching and often humorous events\, Ove’s journey from isola
 tion to connection reminds us of the power of community\, love\, and secon
 d chances. It's a story about how even the most seemingly unapproachable p
 eople can have a profound impact on the world around them.\nFriday June 19
 \n: The Alchemist\nby Paulo Coelho\nThe Alchemist by Paulo Coelho is a sim
 ple yet deeply philosophical novel about following your dreams. It tells t
 he story of Santiago\, a young shepherd who embarks on a journey from Spai
 n to the Egyptian desert in search of treasure. Along the way\, he encount
 ers mentors and challenges that teach him about listening to his heart\, r
 ecognizing opportunity\, and pursuing his “Personal Legend.” Blending 
 elements of fable\, spirituality\, and adventure\, the book explores theme
 s of destiny\, courage\, and the idea that the universe supports those who
  truly commit to their dreams.\nIf you need help getting a copy of the boo
 k or have any questions\, please contact us at sastaff@tpl.ca or 416-393-7
 653. Copies of the monthly titles are available at the circulation desk.
LOCATION:Sanderson\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6962837f91e4576f847bd0ff
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Between the Lines Bookclub
