BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tracey Lindberg: The Cree Word for Love
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T200000
UID:69a0af3c4a9fd98b7f80c93e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Bestselling author Tracey Lindberg shares her stunning new boo
 k exploring love in all its forms in the Cree experience.\nIn\nThe Cree Wo
 rd for Love\n\, author Tracey Lindberg and artist George Littlechild consi
 der a teaching from an Elder that in their culture\, the notion of love as
  constructed in Western society does not exist.\nTogether they have create
 d and curated a collaboration which travels\, season by season\, mirroring
  the four rounds in ceremony\, through the themes of the love within a fam
 ily\, ties of kinship\, desire for romantic love and connection\, strength
  in the face of loss and violence\, and importance of self-love\, as well 
 as\, crucially\, a deeper exploration of the meaning of “all my relation
 s.”\nTracey Lindberg speaks with multidisciplinary artist Jessica Lea Fl
 eming about her new book and considers where we find connection\, strengt
 h\, and the many forms of what it means to live lovingly.\nAbout this even
 t’s guests:\nTracey Lindberg\nJessica Lea Fleming\nRead more:\nThe Cree 
 Word for Love\nBirdie\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nConcourse Event S
 pace\, North York Central Library (5120 Yonge St)\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00p
 m\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket po
 licy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest number of peop
 le have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until
  capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\n***\nThis event 
 is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and interna
 tional authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new book
 s and big ideas.\nIt is also part of our\nIndigenous Heritage series\n\, g
 enerously supported by TD Bank Group\, through the TD Ready Commitment.\n*
 **\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form 
 is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\
 , sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used fo
 r event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants ma
 y be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Questions
  about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerl
 ine staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto
  ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a0af3c4a9fd98b7f80c93e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Anakana Schofield: Library of Brothel
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T200000
UID:69a0af504a9fd98b7f80c941@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Celebrated author Anakana Schofield shares her long-anticipate
 d and wildly-inventive new novel.\nIn\nLibrary of Brothel\ntakes us into a
 n eponymous building where no one has a job\, no one can find a date\, and
  many struggle just to be housed. Rumours swirl around the crumbling struc
 ture and rapacious developers have their eyes on it. But what we find insi
 de is a unique economy: customers take shelter\, workers love their jobs\,
  and each room offers a new kind of intellectual stimulation. How can such
  a precarious place survive?\nAnakana Schofield speaks with journalist and
  critic Michelle Dean about her dazzling new novel\, a cri de coeur for h
 uman connection and the right to meaningful work.\nQ&A and book signing to
  follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nAnak
 ana Schofield\nMichelle Dean\nRead more:\nLibrary of Brothel : A Novel\nBi
 na\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto
  Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00p
 m\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket po
 licy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest number of peop
 le have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until
  capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar:\nBeer/wine 
 available for purchase (debit/credit only).\n***\nThis event is part of ou
 r signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and international author
 s\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books and big ide
 as.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this
  form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librarie
 s Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be u
 sed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registra
 nts may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Que
 stions about how the library handles your information can be directed to A
 nswerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, T
 oronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram & Bluma Appel Salon\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a0af504a9fd98b7f80c941
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Parallel Paths: the Chinese and Jewish Communities in The Ward and
  Beyond
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T193000
UID:69a0bbdc491b809c6f1cbf0d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The civic narratives of Toronto’s Chinese and Jewish communi
 ties have overlapped since the days of The Ward in the early 20th century\
 , through to the present day. In this unique discussion\, aided by artefac
 ts from the Chinese Canadian Archive\, historian and author Arlene Chan an
 d journalist John Lorinc will explore both the pleasure and the importance
  of cultural connection in the world’s most diverse metropolis.\nABOUT T
 HIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\nArlene Chan\nshares her passion for the history\, cul
 ture\, and traditions of the Chinese in Canada in her speaking engagements
 \, essays\, and books\, including\nThe Chinese in Toronto from 1878\n(2011
 ). With a long standing commitment to community work\, she currently serve
 s as the president of the Jean Lumb Foundation.\nJohn Lorinc\nis a Toronto
  freelance journalist who writes about cities\, business\, immigration and
  local history for a range of publications\, including\nThe Globe and Mail
 \, the Toronto Star\nand\nSpacing\n. John co-edited\nThe Ward: The Life an
 d Loss of Toronto’s First Immigrant Neighbourhood\n\, and\nThe Ward Unco
 vered: The Archaeology of Everyday Life\n. He is also the author of\nNo Je
 ws Live Here: A Memoir\n(Coach House Books\, 2024).\n***\nThis event is pa
 rt of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and internationa
 l authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books and
  big ideas.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBeeton Hall\, Toronto Refer
 ence Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 1st floor\nTime:\nDoors open 5:30pm\, Ev
 ent starts 6:30pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\
 nWe oversell these events to make sure that the greatest number of people 
 have an opportunity to attend. Tickets are guaranteed until 6:45pm or unti
 l capacity is reached. Any remaining space will be opened up to the rush l
 ine beginning at 6:45pm. Seating first come\, first served. Tickets do not
  guarantee seats.\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If y
 ou are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request accommodation f
 or this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 a
 t least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformation Collection Notic
 e:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publ
 ic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 2
 0(d). The information will only be used for event and program registration
 \, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contacted to fill out a br
 ief survey following the program\n. Questions about how the library handle
 s your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.c
 a\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, 1st Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a0bbdc491b809c6f1cbf0d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Richard Hell: Godlike
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260515T200000
UID:69a0bc761e64afd01e5e5664@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:It's New York City\, 1972\, and twenty-seven-year old poet Pau
 l Vaughn is feeling old before his time when sixteen-year-old poet R.T. Wo
 de arrives in the city. They begin a tempestuous affair\, dropping acid an
 d crashing parties and perambulating the grit and grime of the East Villag
 e. Paul is in love with T.\, but T. is in love with experience. Their rela
 tionship disintegrates. This is the premise of Richard Hell's\nGodlike\n\,
  a novel of compelling originality and transcendent beauty\, originally pu
 blished in 2005 and now entering the canon of New York Review Classics.\nI
 f the premise sounds familiar\, that's because\nGodlike\ntransposes the no
 torious romance of Paul Verlaine and Arthur Rimbaud to the East Village in
  its squalid\, glorious '70s heyday. The book comprises a version of Paul'
 s 1997 hospital notebooks: diaries amidst poems and essays\, along with\, 
 most pertinently\, the poet's third-person memoir-novelette of his youthfu
 l time with the now-famous T.\nGodlike\nis infused as well with evocations
  – and sometimes actual poems – of many New York poets of the era\, fr
 om Ted Berrigan and Ron Padgett to Edwin Denby and James Schuyler.\nJoin h
 ost Sean Michaels for a season in hell as Richard Hell discusses his begui
 ling masterpiece.\n***\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\nRichard Hell\nwas born
  and grew up in Lexington\, Kentucky\, but dropped out of high school and 
 moved alone to New York at the age of seventeen. He first came to public a
 ttention in the mid-1970s as an originator of punk. In 1984 he retired fro
 m music and returned to his original ambition of writing books. He is the 
 author of several works of fiction\, poetry\, essays\, notebooks\, and aut
 obiography\, including\nThe Voidoid\n\,\nAcross the Years\n\,\nArtifact\n\
 ,\nGo Now\n\,\nHot and Cold\n\,\nI Dreamed I Was a Very Clean Tramp\n\,\nM
 assive Pissed Love\n\, and\nWhat Just Happened\n\, as well as co-author of
  book-length collaborations\, including the collection of poems\nWanna Go 
 Out?\n(published under the heteronym Theresa Stern) with the musician Tom 
 Verlaine and the image-texts of\nPsychopts\nwith the artist Christopher Wo
 ol.\nSean Michaels\nis the award-winning author of the novels\nDo You Reme
 mber Being Born\n\,\nThe Wagers\n\, and\nUs Conductors.\nIn 2003\, he foun
 ded the pioneering mp3 blog\nSaid the Gramophone\n\, and his work has appe
 ared in\nThe New Yorker\, The Guardian\, Rolling Stone\n\, and many others
 . His zines\nIT LASTS FOREVER\nand\nIF YOU'RE FEELING SINISTER\ncan be fou
 nd at bookstores and on photocopy racks in Montreal.\n***\nThis event is p
 art of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and internation
 al authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books an
 d big ideas.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon
 \, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors 
 open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\
 nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to make sure that the greatest n
 umber of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets are guaranteed unti
 l 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Any remaining space will be opened 
 up to the rush line beginning at 6:45pm. Seating first come\, first served
 . Tickets do not guarantee seats.\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to acc
 essibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request
  accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca o
 r 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformation 
 Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by 
 the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)
 \, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and prog
 ram registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contacted 
 to fill out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about how th
 e library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at 
 answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
