BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dystopia Nonfiction Book Club: How Fascism Works
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260511T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260511T153000
UID:69bd9aa74dacf581ff835dec@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us each month to discuss issues in politics and society\,
  and explore how powerful forces are exploiting our world and dystopian im
 aginings are becoming present-day realities.\nMay's book is\nHow Fascism W
 orks: The Politics of Us and Them\nby philosopher Jason Stanley. Copies
  of the book are available to borrow in\nprint\n\,\neBook\n\, and\neAudiob
 ook\nformats. Book club attendees are encouraged to read the book in adv
 ance of the meeting.\nHow Fascism Works\n“identifies ten pillars of fasc
 ist politics—an appeal to the mythic past\, propaganda\, anti-intellectu
 alism\, unreality\, hierarchy\, victimhood\, law and order\, sexual anxiet
 y\, favoring “the heartland\,” and a dismantling of public goods and u
 nions—that amount to an urgent diagnosis of the tactics right-wing polit
 icians use to break down democracies.” By analyzing recent events in Hun
 gary\, Poland\, India\, Myanmar\, and the United States\, among other nati
 ons\, “Stanley uncovers disturbing patterns that are as prevalent today 
 as ever\, and reveals that the stuff of politics—rhetoric and myth—can
  become policy and reality all too quickly. Only by recognizing them\, he 
 argues\, can we begin to resist their most harmful effects and return to d
 emocratic ideals.”\nDrop in. No registration required.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Discussion Room\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69bd9aa74dacf581ff835dec
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dystopia Nonfiction Book Club: Invisible Doctrine
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260608T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260608T153000
UID:69bd9ec48089f23c67638c03@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us each month to discuss issues in politics and society\,
  and explore how powerful forces are exploiting our world and dystopian im
 aginings are becoming present-day realities.\nJune’s book is\nInvisible 
 Doctrine: the Secret History of Neoliberalism\nby journalist George Monbio
 t and filmmaker Peter Hutchison. Copies of the book are available to borr
 ow in\nprint\n\,\neBook\n\, and\neAudiobook\nformats. Book club attendees
  are encouraged to read the book in advance of the meeting.\nInvisible Doc
 trine argues that neoliberalism has been transformed from a “fringe phil
 osophy in the 1930s” to “the dominant ideology of our time”\, an “
 extreme creed” accepted by many as “a kind of natural law” which cha
 mpions “competition as the defining feature of humankind”. “One of t
 he most pernicious effects has been to make our various crises—from clim
 ate disasters to economic crashes\, from the degradation of public service
 s to rampant child poverty—seem unrelated. In fact\, they have all been
  exacerbated by the “invisible doctrine\,” which subordinates democrac
 y to the power of money. Monbiot and Hutchison… trace a direct line from
  neoliberalism to fascism\, which preys on people’s hopelessness and des
 peration.”\nIn addition\, we will be discussing Chapters 4\, 7 & 8\nof\n
 Breaking Free of Neoliberalism: Canada's Challenge: What It Will Take to D
 eal With American Decline\, Inequality and the Climate Crisis\nby sociolog
 ist and former senior civil servant Alex Himmelfarb.\nDrop in. No registra
 tion required.\nPlease note:\nthe Dystopia Nonfiction Book Club will not b
 e meeting in July. Our next meeting is August 17.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Discussion Room\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69bd9ec48089f23c67638c03
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sameer Farooq and Dan Hicks: Restitution and Reimagining Collectio
 ns
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T200000
UID:69c148e4567ba1dd31fe3d6a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL in partnership with the Toronto Biennial of Art and Canada
 <->International\, present artist Sameer Farooq and curator and author Dan
  Hicks in conversation with Tandazani Dhlakama at the Toronto Reference Li
 brary's Appel Salon\nMuseums have long been sites of contested memory\, in
 stitutions that claim to preserve culture while often displaying the spoil
 s of colonialism. Collections assembled through empire sit behind glass\, 
 their violent origins obscured by curatorial labels. Objects torn from the
 ir communities are renamed "artifacts" and their stories rewritten. But a 
 growing movement is asking: who has the right to collect\, to display\, to
  tell these stories? And what would it mean to fundamentally reimagine how
  museums relate to the communities they claim to serve?\nArtist Sameer Far
 ooq’s\,\nFlatbread Library\n\, a large-scale sculpture mapping Toronto's
  diversity through flatbreads from bakeries across the city\, was a stando
 ut of the 2024 Toronto Biennial of Art. His work explores how everyday obj
 ects carry stories of migration\, belonging\, and cultural identity. Oxfor
 d University professor Dan Hicks\, author of the groundbreaking\nThe Bruti
 sh Museums\nand the newly released\nEvery Monument Will Fall: A Story of R
 emembering and Forgetting\n(named one of Art Forum's Books of 2025)\, has 
 become one of the world's leading voices on cultural restitution\, challen
 ging museums to reckon with their colonial foundations. ROM Curator of Glo
 bal Africa Tandazani Dhlakama actively upends traditional collecting pract
 ices as she takes stock of and reimagines the future of the ROM's Global A
 frica collection.\nThis conversation emerges from Farooq and Hicks's recen
 t time together during Farooq’s residency at Worlding\, UK (in collabora
 tion with Canada <-> International)\, where they spent time at the Pitt Ri
 vers Museum examining collections and discussing shared concerns about res
 titution\, cultural ownership\, and the ethics of display. How does the mo
 vement and migration of objects\, both historical and contemporary\, infor
 m how we create museum displays? What does it mean to decolonize not just 
 labels but the fundamental act of collecting itself? How do we move beyond
  simply acknowledging colonial violence to actually returning what was tak
 en and engaging in meaningful reconciliation?\nAs monuments fall and museu
 ms face mounting pressure to reckon with their pasts\, these three visiona
 ries offer crucial perspectives on what comes next: new ways of contributi
 ng to\, building\, and exhibiting collections that honor rather than extra
 ct\, that build relationships rather than reinforce hierarchies\, that ima
 gine museums as sites of repair rather than repositories of theft.\nThe 4t
 h edition of the Toronto Biennial of Art opens on September 26\, 2026.\nAb
 out this event’s guests:\nSameer Farooq\nDan Hicks\nTandazani Dhlakama\n
 Read more:\nEvery Monument Will Fall\nThe Brutish Museums\n***\nPLAN YOUR 
 VISIT:\nLocation:\nConcourse Event Space\, North York Central Library (512
 0 Yonge St)\, Lower Level.\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm
 .\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell thes
 e events to ensure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity 
 to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. S
 eating first come\, first served.\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to acc
 essibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request
  accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca o
 r 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nThis event i
 s part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and internat
 ional authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books
  and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal informa
 tion on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Pub
 lic Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information wi
 ll only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluati
 on. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the 
 program. Questions about how the library handles your information can be d
 irected to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yong
 e Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space\, North York C
 entral Library (5120 Yonge St)\, Lower Level.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c148e4567ba1dd31fe3d6a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Christopher B. Zeichmann: Radical Antiquity
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260505T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260505T200000
UID:69c152a1db57df0442973dd2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL presents historian Christopher Zeichmann in conversation w
 ith Nahlah Ayed at the Toronto Reference Library's Jack Rabinovitch Readin
 g Room.\nIn his groundbreaking book\nRadical Antiquity: Free Love Zoroastr
 ians\, Farming Pirates\, and Ancient Uprisings\n\, historian and religious
  studies scholar Christopher Zeichmann takes us on an uncommon ground-leve
 l tour through the ancient Greco-Roman world. Instead of viewing this peri
 od through the lens of elites\, Zeichmann recovers the stories of escaped 
 slaves\, self-governing pirates\, radical religious sects\, and reclusive 
 communities who rejected the state entirely. From Spartacus and the uprisi
 ngs of the enslaved to "free love" Zoroastrians who practiced radical gend
 er equality\, these communities sought an egalitarian way of life that avo
 ided the coercion\, hierarchy\, and exploitation that characterized the em
 pires around them.\nJoin Toronto Metropolitan University professor Christo
 pher Zeichmann and CBC IDeas host Nahlah Ayed for a revelatory conversatio
 n that radically expands our understanding of the ancient world and what i
 t means to live freely.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available f
 or purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nChristopher B. Zeichmann\nNahl
 ah Ayed\nRead More:\nRadical Antiquity\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\n
 Jack Rabinovitch Reading Room\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Stree
 t)\, 4th floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\
 nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to 
 ensure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. T
 ickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first
  come\, first served\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. I
 f you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request accommodatio
 n for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-709
 9 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nThis event is part of our
 \nOn Civil Society\nseries\, where we host local and international authors
 \, activists and academics in conversation about the most critical topics 
 of our time. Generously supported by the McConnell Foundation and also by 
 Chris M. Reid.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal informat
 ion on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Publ
 ic Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information wil
 l only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluatio
 n. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the p
 rogram. Questions about how the library handles your information can be di
 rected to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge
  Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Jack Rabinovitch Reading Room\, Toron
 to Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 4th floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c152a1db57df0442973dd2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Merilyn Simonds: Walking with Beth
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T200000
UID:69c158ab07a74a47597a6b54@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL presents acclaimed author and Governor General's Award fin
 alist Merilyn Simonds in conversation with journalist and author Moira We
 lsh at the Toronto Reference Library's Jack Rabinovitch Reading Room.\nIn 
 her intimate and profoundly moving book Walking with Beth: Conversations w
 ith My Hundred-Year-Old Friend\, national bestselling author Merilyn Simon
 ds invites us into three years of weekly walks with Beth Robinson\, her ne
 ighbour and friend. When they began in the spring of 2021\, Simonds had ju
 st turned 70\, still active\, still writing\, but entering what struck her
  as a mysterious and frightening stage of life. Beth\, a former dancer and
  occupational therapist who worked until age 99\, was already a centenaria
 n: vibrant\, sharp\, and as awake to the world as someone half her age. Wh
 o better to ask what might come next?\nWhat emerges is neither a conventio
 nal memoir nor a straightforward biography\, but something more fluid: a m
 editation on friendship between women across generations\, on mortality an
 d meaning\, on the passions that sustain us through life's least understoo
 d passage.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\
 nAbout this event’s guests:\nMerilyn Simonds\nRead More:\nWalking With B
 eth\nThe Convict Lover\nAstonishing Lives of Older Women : How to Create P
 leasure Over Peril in Peak Longevity\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nJa
 ck Rabinovitch Reading Room\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)
 \, 4th floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nF
 ree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to en
 sure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tic
 kets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first c
 ome\, first served\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If 
 you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request accommodation 
 for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 
 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nThis event is part of our s
 ignature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and international authors\,
  artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books and big ideas.
 \n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this fo
 rm is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries A
 ct\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used
  for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants
  may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Questi
 ons about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answ
 erline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toro
 nto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Jack Rabinovitch Reading Room\, Toron
 to Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 4th floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c158ab07a74a47597a6b54
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
