BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Anarchist Queen and Her Radiant Protégé: Emma Goldman and Mar
 ie Tiboldo in Interwar Toronto
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260502T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260502T163000
UID:69c7ed7ae2a2952aed0b2ca3@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Please join us for an engaging talk presented by Kensington Ma
 rket Historical Society and Toronto Public Library.\nEmma Goldman was an a
 narchist and anti-war activist who was instrumental in mentoring other act
 ivists during her time in Toronto. After Goldman’s death\, protégés li
 ke Marie Tiboldo continued her work. Join presenters Cynthia Wright and Fr
 anca Iacovetta to learn more about their impact on interwar Toronto.\nRefr
 eshments will be offered following the talk.\nCynthia Wright is Associate 
 Professor in the School of Gender\, Sexuality\, and Women’s Studies at Y
 ork University. Franca Iacovetta is Professor Emerita\, University of Toro
 nto\, and co-editor of Studies in Gender and History at University of Toro
 nto Press.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Room B/C
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c7ed7ae2a2952aed0b2ca3
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Journalism Series with David Silverberg: What you need to know abo
 ut Canadian media today
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260506T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260506T193000
UID:69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This lecture is presented by the North York Central Library's
  2026 Writer in Residence David Silverberg\, whose focus is on Journalism.
 \nEvery journalist should be aware of the shifting landscape in Canadian m
 edia. As many print magazines and regional newspapers shutter for good\, i
 nnovative and bold news networks thriving online are filling the gap. Edit
 ors are looking for articles on topics you wouldn’t read years ago wheth
 er in science\, technology\, the arts or sports. You’ll find out the kin
 d of engaging articles freelance journalists can write for these outlets.\
 nThis lecture will be followed by Q&A\nLearn more about TPL's\nWriter in R
 esidence\nprogram\, including upcoming programs and one-on-one consultatio
 n sessions.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on t
 his form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libra
 ries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only b
 e used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Quest
 ions about how the library handles your information can be directed to Ans
 werline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, 
 Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Journalism Series with David Silverberg: How to pitch your article
  ideas to editors
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T193000
UID:69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f3@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This lecture is presented by the North York Central Library's 
 2026 Writer in Residence David Silverberg\, whose focus is on Journalism.\
 nIf you’ve always wanted to write for a news network\, you’ll want to 
 come to this talk and analysis of what makes a strong pitch. To impress ed
 itors\, a pitch has to include several important journalistic ingredients
 …and your personality. First\, learn how to find story ideas in your com
 munity and beyond. Next\, you’ll be shown how those ideas can turn into 
 compelling pitches. Examples of great (and not so great) pitches will show
  you why certain ideas attract an editor’s attention.\nThis lecture will
  be followed by Q&A\nLearn more about TPL's\nWriter in Residence\nprogram\
 , including upcoming programs and one-on-one consultation sessions.\nInfor
 mation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collec
 ted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, section
 s 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event a
 nd program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the
  library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\n
 answerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8
 .
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f3
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Journalism Series with David Silverberg: Adding vivid details to y
 our non-fiction work (workshop)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260526T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260526T193000
UID:69c81fe8b48451fc3c0bd8f4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This workshop is presented by the North York Central Library's
  2026 Writer in Residence David Silverberg\, whose focus is on Journalism.
 \nFrom hard news articles to creative non-fiction\, specifics matter. Conc
 rete descriptions of your article’s location and its main interviewees w
 ill help immerse readers in your story. In this workshop\, you’ll take p
 art in several exercises designed to bring colour and sensory details to y
 our writing. You’ll also learn from examples written by some of the most
  iconic journalists in the world such as Joan Didion and Pierre Berton.\nT
 his lecture will be followed by Q&A\nLearn more about TPL's\nWriter in Res
 idence\nprogram\, including upcoming programs and one-on-one consultation 
 sessions.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on thi
 s form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librari
 es Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be 
 used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questio
 ns about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answe
