BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Modern Fairy Tales on Film: Sleeping Beauty
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260529T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260529T130000
UID:69ea699fdefec6489cbfc5fc@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In this instalment of Toronto Public Library's ongoing virtual
  series\, host Adam Nayman is joined by critic Tia Glista and writer and a
 ctress Andrea Werhun to explore\nSleeping Beauty\n(2011). A far cry from t
 he Disney version of 1959\, this\nSleeping Beauty\nis the directorial debu
 t of the fascinating Julia Leigh\, who first came to prominence as a novel
 ist. Screened in competition at the Cannes Film Festival\, Leigh's\nSleepi
 ng Beauty\ncentres upon Lucy\, a struggling university student who begins 
 moonlighting as a sex worker of a peculiar type: at a country mansion\, sh
 e is voluntarily sedated\, and sleeps naked while male clients lie beside 
 her. With elements draws from the novels of Yasunari Kawabata and Gabriel 
 García Márquez\,\nSleeping Beauty\nradically reimagines the familiar fab
 le for our time.\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\nAdam Nayman\nis a critic\, t
 eacher\, and author\, whose books include critical surveys of the Coen Bro
 thers\, Paul Thomas Anderson\, and most recently\, in\nMind Games\n\, the 
 filmmaker David Fincher. He is a regular contributor to\nThe Ringer\n\, a 
 lecturer at the University of Toronto's Cinema Studies Institute\, and he 
 has written on film for\nThe New Yorker\nand\nThe New York Times\n.\nTia G
 lista\nis a Toronto-based arts writer and PhD candidate. Her criticism and
  essays on gender in film\, literature\, and art have appeared in\nThe Gua
 rdian\, Los Angeles Review of Books\, Dazed\, Document Journal\,\nand\nEle
 ctric Literature\,\nand she is a founding editor of\nToronto Review.\nAndr
 ea Werhun\nis a writer\, performer\, and producer based in Toronto. She is
  the author of\nModern Whore: A Memoir\n(Strange Light/Penguin Random Hous
 e Canada)\, along with filmmaker Nicole Bazuin\, who adapted the book into
  a hybrid documentary film in which Andrea starred. She is a regular contr
 ibutor to\nThe Globe and Mail\nand CBC Radio\, and is the author of\nPubli
 c Figure\n\, her bi-weekly Substack.\n***\nImportant information for acces
 sing this live virtual event:\nYou must register for a free ticket via Eve
 ntbrite to gain access to this event.\nThis event will be presented in a Z
 oom Webinar. Once registered\, you will receive reminder emails from both 
 Zoom and Eventbrite with links and instructions to access your unique Zoom
  Webinar link to watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, you must be
  signed in to Zoom or Eventbrite with the same email address that you used
  to book your ticket in order to access your unique event link. Unique eve
 nt links can only be open on one device at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at 
 Toronto Public Library\nWe are committed to making our content accessible 
 to as many Torontonians as possible. Please call or email us if you are De
 af or have a disability and would like to request accommodation to partici
 pate in this program. Please let us know as far in advance as possible and
  we will do our best to meet your request. At least three weeks' notice is
  preferred. Phone 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ea699fdefec6489cbfc5fc
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
