BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Author Talk with Steve Burgess: The Philosophy and Practice of Liv
 ing Well on a Small Budget
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T193000
UID:69dbf30666c3387a9d0697c6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Blending personal memoir\, economic philosophy\, and sharp soc
 ial observation\, Vancouver-based author\nSteve Burgess\nwill talk about h
 is new book\,\nCheapskate in Lotusland: The Philosophy and Practice of Li
 ving Well on a Small Budget\,\nand his tips on living well without ever ea
 rning too much.\nSet in Vancouver\, one of North America's most expensive 
 cities and comparable to Toronto\, the book explores frugality as a way of
  life rather than a sacrifice. From mastering grocery store bargains to qu
 estioning the relentless push of consumerism\, Burgess offers lessons in l
 iving comfortably on less—without deprivation or despair\, and that fina
 ncial security isn’t always about numbers—it’s about priorities\, pe
 rspective and knowing the value of a good pair of shoes.\nAbout The Author
 \nSteve Burgess is a writer and broadcaster whose honours include two Cana
 dian National Magazine awards. Burgess is a contributing editor of The Tye
 e and an award-winning documentary director. He is also the author of Rese
 rvations: The Pleasures and Perils of Travel (2024) which was longlisted f
 or the Leacock Medal. His stories have been featured in publications inclu
 ding Reader’s Digest\, Maclean’s\, and The Globe and Mail. He lives in
  Vancouver\, BC.\nRegistration:\nThis virtual event is free to join and wi
 ll take place on\nZoom\n. Register to receive an email with details on ho
 w to join the session.\nThis program will not be recorded.\nCaptions will 
 be available.\nNeed help with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and participating in a Zoo
 m webinar\n-\nZoom Privacy Statement\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto 
 Public Library:\nCheapskate in Lotusland\n(book)\nCheapskate in Lotusland\
 n(ebook)\nOther books available at TPL\nby Steve Burgess.\nInformation Col
 lection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the
  Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 
 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program
  registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library h
 andles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline
 @tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69dbf30666c3387a9d0697c6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Writers Collective of Canada (WCC): Writing Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260606T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260606T163000
UID:69ee56c31e64afd01e6bd84d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:When we write with others\, we discover ourselves\n.\nWriters 
 Collective of Canada (WCC) workshops offer a brave creative space where al
 l are welcomed to write and explore their story in a space free of critiqu
 e.\nThe workshops are led by trained volunteer facilitators who also act a
 s participants. The group writes in response to prompts\, is invited to sh
 are their first draft writing\, and offers affirming feedback to one anoth
 er.\nWhile the focus is on the process of writing rather than a finished p
 roduct\, the stories that emerge in WCC workshops are magnificent. In shar
 ing and listening to one another's stories\, participants find connection 
 with self and others.\nNo experience required. Beginners are welcome. All 
 materials are provided.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal info
 rmation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the 
 Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information
  will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evalu
 ation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be dir
 ected to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yon
 ge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Morningside\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ee56c31e64afd01e6bd84d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Finding a Job with ACCES Employment
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T193000
UID:69ebdb675c04bc0fc0636d6b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Effective Resume Development\nThis workshop focuses on buildin
 g a strong\, tailored resume for the Canadian job market. Participants wil
 l learn how to structure their resume\, highlight transferable skills\, an
 d align their experience with job postings. Time will be included for ques
 tions and light resume feedback.\nParticipants are encouraged (but not req
 uired) to bring a copy of their resume (digital or printed) for feedback.\
 nDrop-In. Space is limited.
LOCATION:Weston\, Program Room\, Basement Level
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ebdb675c04bc0fc0636d6b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google AI Essentials: Online Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260506T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260506T120000
UID:69d93eb4129f12743837bc97@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Curious about artificial intelligence or want to learn practic
 al AI skills for work and personal use? Join our staff-facilitated learnin
 g circle to complete the self-paced Google AI Essentials specialization.\n
 This 10-hour introductory-level specialization\, offered through Coursera\
 , is available for free to learning circle participants. It is made up of 
 five courses\, each containing four units that consist of short videos\, a
 rticles\, assignments and a short multiple-choice quiz.\nBy completing the
  specialization\,\nyou will learn:\nHow AI works\nHow to write effective p
 rompts\nHow to use generative AI tools responsibly\nHow to identify AI's p
 otential biases and avoid harm\nHow to stay up to date with AI news\nAfter
  you complete the course\, you earn a Google certificate and a badge to sh
 are with others\nLearning Circles are study groups guided by\nlibrary staf
 f for people who want to complete the Google AI Essentials specialization 
 together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work together thr
 ough course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit to attending a
 ll four learning circle sessions.\nNo AI experience is required.\nHow to J
 oi\nn a Learning Circle:\nRegister once on the first session of this event
  series\, in order to attend all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a\nvalid e
 mail address\n.\nA valid TPL library card is required for online circles
 .\nAttend all four sessions (online via Zoom\, date: May 6\, May 13\, May 
 20 and May 27)\nRegistration is a two-step procedure. First\, participants
  must complete their registration through this page. After this step\, lib
 rary staff will follow up to request additional information in order to co
 mplete the registration.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal inf
 ormation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the
  Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informatio
 n will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and eval
 uation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be di
 rected to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yo
 nge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69d93eb4129f12743837bc97
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
