BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:10 Tips To Find Your Next TPL Program
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T130000
UID:69efb03e129f1274383b28ff@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:From author talks to nature walks\, family story times to seni
 ors' socials\, 3D printing to digital creations\, TPL is more than books\,
  with something for everyone!\nStarting June 1\, library programs will no 
 longer be posted on Eventbrite. Our TPL website is the go-to place to brow
 se TPL programs\, register\, receive reminders\, and keep track of your bo
 okings.\nJoin us for this staff-led session to help you easily find your n
 ext library program online or in-person at your local branch.\nAt this 1-h
 our session\, learn how to:\nFind programs that match your interests\nRegi
 ster for a program\, join a waitlist\, and cancel your registration\nTrack
  your programs and registrations via My Account\nDiscover new library init
 iatives and local branch events\n...and more!\nWith thousands of free libr
 ary programs available year-round\, TPL programs offers endless opportunit
 ies to connect\, learn\, and grow together!\nRegistration:\nThis virtual e
 vent is free to join and will take place on\nZoom\n. Register to receive 
 an email with details on how to join the session. No library card required
 .\nThis program will not be recorded.\nCaptions will be available.\nNeed h
 elp with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and participating in a Zoom webinar\n-\nZoom Pr
 ivacy Statement\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information 
 on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public L
 ibraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will on
 ly be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Q
 uestions about how the library handles your information can be directed to
  Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Stree
 t\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69efb03e129f1274383b28ff
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Movie Matinee: Fight Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260530T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260530T163000
UID:69efc67ffb87a3c2e2ac34b7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an afternoon screening of\nFight Club\n(1999).\nA 
 troubled office worker meets a charismatic soap salesman\, and together t
 hey form an underground club for men- Fight Club. The club soon spirals ou
 t of control with members challenging society through acts of violence an
 d vandalism.\nBased on the book\nby Chuck Palahniuk\, Fight Club is a sha
 rp satire on consumerism and toxic masculinity\, and remains relevant more
  than 25 years after it's release.\nFilm trailer\nThis film is rated R. R
 untime: 2h 19m\nScreening with English closed-captions (CC) enabled.
LOCATION:Pape/Danforth\, 2nd Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69efc67ffb87a3c2e2ac34b7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Master Your Taxes:  A Guide to Keeping the CRA Happy for Entrepren
 eurs\, Freelancers\, and Self-employed Canadians
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260505T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260505T193000
UID:69cdb092f29b90618efd229c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:As shifts in the economy and available technologies change the
  reality of work\, more and more Canadians are dipping into the world of s
 elf-employment\, consulting and freelancing. Starting a new endeavor can b
 e thrilling\, but it comes with challenges\, as well. One of those is the 
 new complexity of the unique tax issues that come with running a business.
 \nJoin us for an entertaining and information-packed presentation by Sunn
 y Widerman about the financial and tax aspects of running your own busine
 ss.\nNote: This is\nnot\na tax clinic.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, 1st Fl
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69cdb092f29b90618efd229c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Author Talk: Chop Suey Nation and Other Stories from Canada’s Ch
 inese Restaurants with Ann Hui
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T200000
UID:69f0b6d6129f1274383b4833@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join award-winning journalist\nAnn Hui\n\, author of\nChop Sue
 y Nation\n: The Legion Cafe and Other Stories from Canada’s Chinese Rest
 aurants\, who will share the remarkable journey that began as a cross‑co
 untry reporting assignment and evolved into a deeply personal exploration 
 of family history\, Chinese values intertwined with entrepreneurialism\, a
 nd the fascinating history behind "chop suey" cuisine.\nHui will be in con
 versation with\nJeanette Liu\n\, co-owner (along with her sister) of\nYueh
  Tung Restaurant\n\, the oldest Hakka Chinese restaurant in Canada and one
  of the last remaining pillars of Toronto’s original Chinatown.\nFree pr
 ogram. Please register. The presentation will include an audience Q&A.\nP
 resented as part of Asian Heritage Month Celebrations and TPL's Small Busi
 ness Networking (SBN) series\, designed to spark connection\, conversation
 \, and possibility for Toronto’s diverse entrepreneurial community. Stic
 k around after the talk for networking and mingling\, refreshments\, and a
  chance to meet like-minded peers.\nThis program is generously supported t
 hrough TPL's\nNewcomer Entrepreneurship Initiative\n.\nPlace a hold or bor
 row from Toronto Public Library:\nChop Suey Nation\n(book)\nChop Suey Nati
 on\n(ebook)\nRead about Jeanette Liu and Yueh Tung Restaurant:\nhttps://w
 ww.theglobeandmail.com/opinion/article-a-wok-to-remember-saving-our-parent
 s-legendary-restaurant-in-torontos/\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe p
 ersonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Librar
 y under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The
  information will only be used for event and program registration\, delive
 ry and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informatio
 n can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-59
 81\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Basement Room B/C
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f0b6d6129f1274383b4833
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Investigative Tension: Compose True Crime Soundtracks with AI Tool
 s
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T180000
UID:69a202588089f23c675eb015@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:True crime is one of the most popular genres in podcasts\, doc
 umentaries\, and streaming television - and the music that drives these st
 ories plays a huge role in building suspense and keeping audiences on the 
 edge of their seats.\nIn this hands-on workshop\, you'll break down what m
 akes an effective true crime soundtrack tick\, then use AI tools to compos
 e your own investigative tension track from scratch. The focus will be on 
 using AI as an assistant while keeping you in the driver's seat as the cre
 ative decision-maker. No prior music or programming experience is require
 d.\nRegistration is required.\nThis program is part of the Innovator in Re
 sidence (AI and Music Production) program\, which is designed to help begi
 nner and intermediate learners build knowledge and creative skills and get
  hands-on with current AI tools in music production and learn about the hi
 story\, copyright and ethics of generative music. For more information and
